# tests/assertions/kreset.rxt — module `assertions`, [M6.2] WAVE E: `\K`,
# which RESETS THE REPORTED START of the match.
#
# THE ORACLE RULE IS WAVE D'S, FOR WAVE D'S REASON: **python3 `re` HAS NO `\K`
# AT ALL.** `re.compile(r"a\Kb")` raises `error: bad escape \K`, and there is
# no flag, dialect or rewriting that expresses it — python has no way to move
# a match's reported start from inside a pattern. So this whole file is
# `# pcre2-only`, wholesale and per block, and rides
# tests/assertions/verify_pcre2.py against libpcre2. That makes it the FOURTH
# place in this module where the base-tier oracle cannot answer, and the
# SECOND total exclusion: `\Z` (wave A) is answered WRONGLY, `(?m)^` (wave C)
# DIFFERENTLY, `\G` (wave D) not at all, and `\K` not at all.
# (docs/dev/upstream_issues.md U11d.)
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS ABOUT, in one sentence: `\K` is the only construct in the
# module that is not an assertion. It asks nothing, it cannot fail, and it has
# a SIDE EFFECT — so unlike every other file here, the interesting cells are
# not "does it hold at this position" but "which of several candidate writes
# SURVIVED to the end of the winning path".
#
# THE THREE FAMILIES THAT CARRY THE FILE, and none of them is decoration:
#
#   - **the UNDO cells (sections 3 and 4)**. `\K`'s write is TRAILED, and the
#     cells that need it are the ones where a `\K` is crossed on a path that
#     then LOSES. `(?:a\K|ax)c` on "axc" is (0,3): the `a\K` branch writes 1,
#     the follow fails, and the write must be GONE when `ax` wins. An
#     implementation that wrote `caps[0][0]` without trailing it answers
#     (1,3), and nothing else in this directory would notice.
#   - **the NOT-CROSSED cells (section 5)**. `(?:a\K)?b` on "b" is (0,1),
#     because no `\K` was passed. That is not a defensive default in the
#     emitter, it is `\K`'s semantics, and the cells are what stop the
#     "no `\K` crossed" sentinel being confused with a position.
#   - **the START == END cells (section 6)**. `ab\K` on "ab" is (2,2) — a
#     reported span of length ZERO after two bytes were consumed. This is the
#     shape that makes the match-here entries' RETURN VALUE a different
#     question from their reported span (assertions_design.md §6.3 rule 3),
#     and it is why a caller that advanced by `caps[0][1] - caps[0][0]` would
#     never move. No `.rxt` block can drive an entry, so the entries
#     themselves are tests/assertions/kreset_entries.c; these cells pin the
#     property the entries are about.
#
# `ms`/`ns` CELLS ARE PRESENT THROUGHOUT AND SWEEP EVERY startpos, on wave B's
# and wave D's precedent but for a THIRD reason: the reported start is the one
# quantity in the whole match API that `\K` can move, so a cell at startpos > 0
# is what distinguishes "the VM reported where it crossed the `\K`" from "the
# prefilter's span start was written out" — the two agree at every position
# only if `\K` is being ignored (assertions_design.md §6.3 rule 1). Under the
# hybrid the prefilter's start IS the reverse pass's answer, so these cells are
# that rule's behavioural half; the structural half is
# tests/codegen/run_codegen_tests.sh's `[M6.2-KRESET]` block.
#
# THE GRAMMAR REFUSALS are at the end, with the module ENABLED: `\K*` `\K+`
# `\K?` `\K{2}` `a\K*` are PCRE2 error 109 and `(\K)*` compiles — `\A`/`\z`/
# `\b`/`\G`'s table cell for cell, measured against libpcre2 10.46 by this
# wave, which is why `\K` joins `pcrec_is_bare_anchor` rather than earning a
# rule of its own.
#
# EVERY EXPECTATION BELOW WAS PRODUCED BY libpcre2 10.46 (through
# tests/fuzz/pcre2_oracle, options = 0) rather than written by hand.


# ======================================================================
# 1. `\K` BARE, AND THE TWO TRIVIAL PLACEMENTS. `\K` alone matches the empty
# string everywhere, exactly as `(?:)` does — which is the point: `\K` at the
# START of a pattern and `\K` alone change NOTHING, so these blocks are the
# control that says the construct is not simply an anchor in disguise. The
# first cell that moves is `a\K` in section 2's neighbour below.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern \K
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "a" 0 0
ms 1 "a" 1 1
m "ab" 0 0
ms 1 "ab" 1 1
ms 2 "ab" 2 2
m "abc" 0 0
ms 1 "abc" 1 1
ms 2 "abc" 2 2
ms 3 "abc" 3 3

# pcre2-only
pattern \Ka
features assertions
m "a" 0 1
ns 1 "a"
m "ab" 0 1
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xa" 1 2
ms 1 "xa" 1 2
ns 2 "xa"
m "xxa" 2 3
ms 1 "xxa" 2 3
ms 2 "xxa" 2 3
ns 3 "xxa"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\K
features assertions
m "a" 1 1
ns 1 "a"
m "ab" 1 1
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xa" 2 2
ms 1 "xa" 2 2
ns 2 "xa"
m "xxa" 3 3
ms 1 "xxa" 3 3
ms 2 "xxa" 3 3
ns 3 "xxa"

# ======================================================================
# 2. THE BASIC RESET, and LAST CROSSING WINS. `a\Kb` on "ab" is (1,2): two
# bytes are consumed from 0 and one byte is reported. `a\Kb\Kc` on "abc" is
# (2,3) — the SECOND `\K` — which is what makes "the reported start is where
# the winning path last crossed a \K" a testable sentence rather than a
# paraphrase of "after the \K". The `\Ka\Kb` and `a\K\Kb` blocks are the same
# fact at the two degenerate placements.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
m "xxab" 3 4
ms 1 "xxab" 3 4
ms 2 "xxab" 3 4
ns 3 "xxab"
ns 4 "xxab"
n "ba"
ns 1 "ba"
ns 2 "ba"
n "a"
ns 1 "a"
n "b"
ns 1 "b"

# pcre2-only
pattern \Kab
features assertions
m "ab" 0 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 1 3
ms 1 "xab" 1 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
m "abab" 0 2
ms 1 "abab" 2 4
ms 2 "abab" 2 4
ns 3 "abab"
ns 4 "abab"

# pcre2-only
pattern ab\K
features assertions
m "ab" 2 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 3 3
ms 1 "xab" 3 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
m "abab" 2 2
ms 1 "abab" 4 4
ms 2 "abab" 4 4
ns 3 "abab"
ns 4 "abab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb\Kc
features assertions
m "abc" 2 3
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "xabc" 3 4
ms 1 "xabc" 3 4
ns 2 "xabc"
ns 3 "xabc"
ns 4 "xabc"

# pcre2-only
pattern \Ka\Kb
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\K\Kb
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# ======================================================================
# 3. THE UNDO, ACROSS AN ALTERNATION — the first of the two families that
# need the write to be TRAILED, and the cheaper one to reason about.
#
# `(?:a\K|ax)c` on "axc" is the section's spine. Leftmost-first tries `a\K`
# first: it matches "a", writes the reported start as 1, and then `c` fails at
# position 1. The `ax` branch wins, and PCRE2 reports (0,3) — so the write of
# 1 must be UNDONE, not merely overwritten, because the winning path never
# crosses a `\K` at all. An emitter that assigned `caps[0][0]` directly
# answers (1,3) here and is right on every cell in sections 1 and 2.
#
# `(?:ab\K|a)b` on "ab" is the same requirement with the loser writing a
# LARGER position than the winner's start (2 against 0), which is the
# direction a "keep the maximum" shortcut would get wrong.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K|ax)c
features assertions
m "axc" 0 3
ns 1 "axc"
ns 2 "axc"
ns 3 "axc"
m "ac" 1 2
ns 1 "ac"
ns 2 "ac"
m "xaxc" 1 4
ms 1 "xaxc" 1 4
ns 2 "xaxc"
ns 3 "xaxc"
ns 4 "xaxc"
m "xac" 2 3
ms 1 "xac" 2 3
ns 2 "xac"
ns 3 "xac"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:ab\K|a)b
features assertions
m "ab" 0 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "abb" 2 3
ns 1 "abb"
ns 2 "abb"
ns 3 "abb"
m "xab" 1 3
ms 1 "xab" 1 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K|b)c
features assertions
m "bc" 0 2
ns 1 "bc"
ns 2 "bc"
m "ac" 1 2
ns 1 "ac"
ns 2 "ac"
m "xbc" 1 3
ms 1 "xbc" 1 3
ns 2 "xbc"
ns 3 "xbc"
m "xac" 2 3
ms 1 "xac" 2 3
ns 2 "xac"
ns 3 "xac"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\Kb|ab\Kc)d
features assertions
m "abd" 1 3
ns 1 "abd"
ns 2 "abd"
ns 3 "abd"
m "abcd" 2 4
ns 1 "abcd"
ns 2 "abcd"
ns 3 "abcd"
ns 4 "abcd"
m "xabd" 2 4
ms 1 "xabd" 2 4
ns 2 "xabd"
ns 3 "xabd"
ns 4 "xabd"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb|c
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "c" 0 1
ns 1 "c"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
m "xc" 1 2
ms 1 "xc" 1 2
ns 2 "xc"

# ======================================================================
# 4. THE UNDO, INSIDE A QUANTIFIER — assertions_design.md §10's NAMED case,
# and the one where the trail has to restore a PREVIOUS `\K` rather than the
# absence of one.
#
# `(?:a\K)*ab` on "aaab" is (2,4), and the derivation is the test: the greedy
# loop takes three iterations, writing 1, then 2, then 3; `ab` then needs "ab"
# at 3 and finds only "b"; the loop retreats to TWO iterations and `ab`
# matches at 2. So the write of 3 must be gone AND the write of 2 must stand.
# A trail that CLEARED on undo instead of restoring the previous value would
# answer (0,4) here — right start for the wrong reason on `(?:a\K)*b`, wrong
# outright on this one.
#
# `(?:a\Kb)*c` on "ababc" is (3,5): two iterations, the second `\K` at 3.
# `(?:a\K){2,}b` pins the same mechanism under a minimum count, where the
# retreat has a floor it must not go below.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K)*ab
features assertions
m "aaab" 2 4
ms 1 "aaab" 2 4
ms 2 "aaab" 2 4
ns 3 "aaab"
ns 4 "aaab"
m "ab" 0 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "aab" 1 3
ms 1 "aab" 1 3
ns 2 "aab"
ns 3 "aab"
m "xaaab" 3 5
ms 1 "xaaab" 3 5
ms 2 "xaaab" 3 5
ms 3 "xaaab" 3 5
ns 4 "xaaab"
ns 5 "xaaab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K)*b
features assertions
m "aaab" 3 4
ms 1 "aaab" 3 4
ms 2 "aaab" 3 4
ms 3 "aaab" 3 4
ns 4 "aaab"
m "b" 0 1
ns 1 "b"
m "ab" 1 2
ms 1 "ab" 1 2
ns 2 "ab"
m "xaaab" 4 5
ms 1 "xaaab" 4 5
ms 2 "xaaab" 4 5
ms 3 "xaaab" 4 5
ms 4 "xaaab" 4 5
ns 5 "xaaab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\Kb)*c
features assertions
m "ababc" 3 5
ms 1 "ababc" 3 5
ms 2 "ababc" 3 5
ms 3 "ababc" 4 5
ms 4 "ababc" 4 5
ns 5 "ababc"
m "c" 0 1
ns 1 "c"
m "abc" 1 3
ms 1 "abc" 2 3
ms 2 "abc" 2 3
ns 3 "abc"
m "xababc" 4 6
ms 1 "xababc" 4 6
ms 2 "xababc" 4 6
ms 3 "xababc" 4 6
ms 4 "xababc" 5 6
ms 5 "xababc" 5 6
ns 6 "xababc"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K){2,}b
features assertions
m "aaab" 3 4
ms 1 "aaab" 3 4
ns 2 "aaab"
ns 3 "aaab"
ns 4 "aaab"
m "aab" 2 3
ns 1 "aab"
ns 2 "aab"
ns 3 "aab"
n "ab"
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K){1,3}b
features assertions
m "aaaab" 4 5
ms 1 "aaaab" 4 5
ms 2 "aaaab" 4 5
ms 3 "aaaab" 4 5
ns 4 "aaaab"
ns 5 "aaaab"
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "aab" 2 3
ms 1 "aab" 2 3
ns 2 "aab"
ns 3 "aab"

# ======================================================================
# 5. THE `\K` NOT CROSSED — the population for "no `\K` on this path", which
# in the emitted artifact is the per-search PCREC_UNSET the trail restores.
#
# `(?:a\K)?b` is the pair that matters and it must be read as a pair: on "ab"
# it is (1,2) and on "b" it is (0,1). Same pattern, same artifact, one cell
# through the `\K` and one around it. A build that treated "no `\K` crossed"
# as an error, or that left a stale write from a previous ATTEMPT, disagrees
# on exactly one of the two.
#
# `(?:a\K|b)+c` sweeps the same question inside a loop where the branch
# choice varies per iteration, so the final answer depends on which branch
# the LAST iteration took rather than on whether any iteration took it.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K)?b
features assertions
m "b" 0 1
ns 1 "b"
m "ab" 1 2
ms 1 "ab" 1 2
ns 2 "ab"
m "xb" 1 2
ms 1 "xb" 1 2
ns 2 "xb"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ms 2 "xab" 2 3
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:\Ka)?b
features assertions
m "b" 0 1
ns 1 "b"
m "ab" 0 2
ms 1 "ab" 1 2
ns 2 "ab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K|b)+c
features assertions
m "abc" 1 3
ms 1 "abc" 1 3
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "bac" 2 3
ms 1 "bac" 2 3
ns 2 "bac"
ns 3 "bac"
m "bbc" 0 3
ms 1 "bbc" 1 3
ns 2 "bbc"
ns 3 "bbc"
m "aac" 2 3
ms 1 "aac" 2 3
ns 2 "aac"
ns 3 "aac"

# ======================================================================
# 6. REPORTED START == REPORTED END, WITH BYTES CONSUMED. `ab\K` on "ab" is
# (2,2). Nothing else in this tree produces a zero-length reported span from
# a match that consumed two bytes, and it is the shape assertions_design.md
# §6.3 rule 3 is about: the match-here entry's RETURN is the consumed length
# (2), not `caps[0][1] - caps[0][0]` (0), and a D38 callout advancing by the
# latter would never move.
#
# `\Ka*` and `a*\K` are the two ends of the same idea against a greedy loop:
# the first reports the whole run, the second reports its end.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern ab\Kc?
features assertions
m "ab" 2 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "abc" 2 3
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "xab" 3 3
ms 1 "xab" 3 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
m "abab" 2 2
ms 1 "abab" 4 4
ms 2 "abab" 4 4
ns 3 "abab"
ns 4 "abab"

# pcre2-only
pattern \Ka*
features assertions
m "aaa" 0 3
ms 1 "aaa" 1 3
ms 2 "aaa" 2 3
ms 3 "aaa" 3 3
m "" 0 0
m "xaaa" 0 0
ms 1 "xaaa" 1 4
ms 2 "xaaa" 2 4
ms 3 "xaaa" 3 4
ms 4 "xaaa" 4 4

# pcre2-only
pattern a*\K
features assertions
m "aaa" 3 3
ms 1 "aaa" 3 3
ms 2 "aaa" 3 3
ms 3 "aaa" 3 3
m "" 0 0
m "xaaa" 0 0
ms 1 "xaaa" 4 4
ms 2 "xaaa" 4 4
ms 3 "xaaa" 4 4
ms 4 "xaaa" 4 4

# ======================================================================
# 7. `\K` WITH CAPTURE GROUPS, which is where the two provenances have to
# stay separate. Group 0's start comes from the `\K` write; groups 1..n come
# from their own slots and are untouched by it. `(a)\K(b)` on "ab" reports
# the whole match as (1,2) while group 1 is (0,1) — a group that STARTS
# BEFORE the reported match start, which is legal and is the cell that would
# fail if the reported start were derived from the groups or vice versa.
#
# The `g` lines here are LIVE, never `gp`: `\K` forces the VM
# (src/opt/select_engine.c), so every artifact in this section has a real
# RX_NCAPS covering every slot asserted.
#
# `((a)\K|(b))c` is the composition of section 3's undo with capture
# provenance: on "bc" the `\K`-bearing branch loses, so both the reported
# start AND group 2 must come back unset.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (a)\K(b)
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\K(b)
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (a\K)(b)
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
g 0 2 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern (a\K)*b
features assertions
m "aaab" 3 4
g 0 3 4
g 1 2 3
ms 1 "aaab" 3 4
g 0 3 4
g 1 2 3
ms 2 "aaab" 3 4
g 0 3 4
g 1 2 3
ms 3 "aaab" 3 4
g 0 3 4
g 1 -1 -1
ns 4 "aaab"
m "b" 0 1
g 0 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
ns 1 "b"
m "ab" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 0 1
ms 1 "ab" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 -1 -1
ns 2 "ab"

# pcre2-only
pattern ((a)\K|(b))c
features assertions
m "ac" 1 2
g 0 1 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 0 1
g 3 -1 -1
ns 1 "ac"
ns 2 "ac"
m "bc" 0 2
g 0 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 0 1
ns 1 "bc"
ns 2 "bc"

# ======================================================================
# 8. `\K` COMPOSED WITH THE MODULE'S OTHER SEVEN CONSTRUCTS. Every wave
# before this one added a question about a position; `\K` adds a write, so
# the composition question is whether the write moves anything the other
# constructs read. It does not, and these blocks are what says so — `\A` and
# `(?m)^` are still about where matching BEGAN, not where reporting begins.
#
# `\Ga\Kb` is the sharpest of them, because it is the only pattern in the
# module carrying BOTH of the two constructs that need something from outside
# `(s, n, pos)`: `\G` needs the `<prefix>_startpos` parameter wave D added,
# and `\K` needs the trailed slot this wave adds. Its startpos sweep is where
# the two would collide if either had been threaded through the other's
# mechanism.
#
# `a\Kb\Z` and `a\Kb$` are here for a second reason: `\Z`/`$` are the two
# spellings the D47.5 possessification gate reasons about, and a `\K` in front
# of one is a shape that gate has never seen.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern \Aa\Kb
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
n "xab"
ns 1 "xab"
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb\z
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
n "abc"
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb\Z
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "ab\n" 1 2
ns 1 "ab\n"
ns 2 "ab\n"
ns 3 "ab\n"
n "abc"
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb$
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "ab\n" 1 2
ns 1 "ab\n"
ns 2 "ab\n"
ns 3 "ab\n"
n "abc"
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"

# pcre2-only
pattern \ba\Kb\b
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m " ab " 2 3
ms 1 " ab " 2 3
ns 2 " ab "
ns 3 " ab "
ns 4 " ab "
n "xab"
ns 1 "xab"
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\K\bb
features assertions
n "ab"
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
n "a b"
ns 1 "a b"
ns 2 "a b"
ns 3 "a b"

# pcre2-only
pattern \Ga\Kb
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
n "xab"
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
n "xxab"
ns 1 "xxab"
ms 2 "xxab" 3 4
ns 3 "xxab"
ns 4 "xxab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb|\Gc
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "c" 0 1
ns 1 "c"
m "xab" 2 3
ms 1 "xab" 2 3
ns 2 "xab"
ns 3 "xab"
n "xc"
ms 1 "xc" 1 2
ns 2 "xc"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?m)^a\Kb
features assertions,modifiers
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "x\nab" 3 4
ms 1 "x\nab" 3 4
ms 2 "x\nab" 3 4
ns 3 "x\nab"
ns 4 "x\nab"
m "x\nabc" 3 4
ms 1 "x\nabc" 3 4
ms 2 "x\nabc" 3 4
ns 3 "x\nabc"
ns 4 "x\nabc"
ns 5 "x\nabc"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb(?m)$
features assertions,modifiers
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "ab\nc" 1 2
ns 1 "ab\nc"
ns 2 "ab\nc"
ns 3 "ab\nc"
ns 4 "ab\nc"
m "xab\n" 2 3
ms 1 "xab\n" 2 3
ns 2 "xab\n"
ns 3 "xab\n"
ns 4 "xab\n"

# ======================================================================
# 9. QUANTIFIERS AROUND THE `\K` RATHER THAN THROUGH IT — the cells where the
# emitter's RUNG choices are what could go wrong rather than the trail.
#
# `a{2,4}\Kb` and `(?:ab)+\Kc` put a `\K` in a quantifier's FOLLOW, where the
# possessification analysis has to treat it as transparent (it cannot fail, so
# it can never be the reason a retreat is needed) without treating the loop
# itself as `\K`-free. `[ab]*\K[bc]+` is the ambiguous-boundary shape R30
# measured six cells of when it withdrew §6.1's ambiguity argument: the
# `\K` position is unique because leftmost-first is a total order, not because
# the boundary is unambiguous.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern a{2,4}\Kb
features assertions
m "aab" 2 3
ns 1 "aab"
ns 2 "aab"
ns 3 "aab"
m "aaab" 3 4
ms 1 "aaab" 3 4
ns 2 "aaab"
ns 3 "aaab"
ns 4 "aaab"
m "aaaab" 4 5
ms 1 "aaaab" 4 5
ms 2 "aaaab" 4 5
ns 3 "aaaab"
ns 4 "aaaab"
ns 5 "aaaab"
n "ab"
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"

# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb{1,3}
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "abbb" 1 4
ns 1 "abbb"
ns 2 "abbb"
ns 3 "abbb"
ns 4 "abbb"
m "xabb" 2 4
ms 1 "xabb" 2 4
ns 2 "xabb"
ns 3 "xabb"
ns 4 "xabb"

# pcre2-only
pattern [ab]*\K[bc]+
features assertions
m "abc" 2 3
ms 1 "abc" 2 3
ms 2 "abc" 2 3
ns 3 "abc"
m "aabbcc" 4 6
ms 1 "aabbcc" 4 6
ms 2 "aabbcc" 4 6
ms 3 "aabbcc" 4 6
ms 4 "aabbcc" 4 6
ms 5 "aabbcc" 5 6
ns 6 "aabbcc"
m "bc" 1 2
ms 1 "bc" 1 2
ns 2 "bc"
m "c" 0 1
ns 1 "c"

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:ab)+\Kc
features assertions
m "abc" 2 3
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "ababc" 4 5
ms 1 "ababc" 4 5
ms 2 "ababc" 4 5
ns 3 "ababc"
ns 4 "ababc"
ns 5 "ababc"
m "xababc" 5 6
ms 1 "xababc" 5 6
ms 2 "xababc" 5 6
ms 3 "xababc" 5 6
ns 4 "xababc"
ns 5 "xababc"
ns 6 "xababc"

# ======================================================================
# 11. THE TRAIL CAPACITY — DEEP quantifiers, and the ONE thing `\K` is not
# free for.
#
# Every other assertion in this module emits a test and costs the capacity
# analysis nothing. `\K` emits a TRAILED SLOT WRITE, so `vm_cost` must charge
# one trail entry per emitted `\K`, multiplied by the enclosing quantifier
# exactly as A_CAP's two writes are. Getting that wrong is not a missed
# optimisation: `trail_frames` comes out one entry short per `\K` on the
# deepest path and the artifact returns PCREC_ERR_FRAMES on a pattern it can
# match.
#
# THE SHALLOW CELLS ABOVE CANNOT SEE IT. Measured on a scratch build with the
# charge removed: `a\Kb` still compiles and still answers, because a
# one-write pattern fits the capacity anyway; `(?:a\K){0,10}ab` declares 3
# trail entries instead of 13 and returns `frames` from eleven `a`s onward.
# So the subjects below are chosen to exceed the count, not to be long — a
# subject that stops short of the repeat's maximum never fills the trail and
# these cells would pass against the defect.
#
# (Measured under the removed charge: 25 of this file's cases fail, all of
# them here and in section 4. The corpus catches it; nothing else does.)
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K){0,10}ab
features assertions
m "ab" 0 2
m "aab" 1 3
m "aaab" 2 4
m "aaaaaaaaaaab" 10 12
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab" 15 17
m "aaaaaaaaaaaab" 11 13
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab" 20 22

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\K){0,10}b
features assertions
m "ab" 1 2
m "aaaaaaaaaaab" 11 12
m "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab" 16 17
m "b" 0 1

# pcre2-only
pattern (?:a\Kb){0,8}c
features assertions
m "abc" 1 3
m "ababababababababc" 15 17
m "c" 0 1
m "ababababababababababc" 19 21


# ======================================================================
# 10. THE GRAMMAR, WITH THE MODULE ENABLED. `\K` is zero-width and NOT a
# repeatable item — PCRE2 error 109 — while a GROUPED `\K` compiles, matching
# `\A`/`\z`/`\b`/`\G` exactly. Both halves are here, because a rule tested in
# one direction is not tested.
#
# `(\K)*` is the accept half and it is worth reading as more than a syntax
# cell: the group is entered zero or more times, so it exercises `\K`'s write
# under the empty-iteration rule as well as under the grammar.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (\K)*
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "a" 0 0
m "ab" 0 0
ms 1 "ab" 1 1

pattern \K*
features assertions
perr

pattern \K+
features assertions
perr

pattern \K?
features assertions
perr

pattern \K{2}
features assertions
perr

pattern a\K*
features assertions
perr

pattern [\K]
features assertions
perr

